{
  "concept": "Diffusion is the net movement of gas molecules down a concentration gradient until uniform, and lighter molecules (H2) spread faster than heavier ones (O2), the qualitative basis of Graham's law.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "left circular bulb (initially H2 side)",
    "right circular bulb (initially O2 side)",
    "short connecting neck/tube between the bulbs",
    "stopcock valve with handle on the neck (closed vs open orientation)",
    "white dots representing H2 molecules",
    "red dots representing O2 molecules",
    "label 'H2' above the left bulb",
    "label 'O2' above the right bulb",
    "caption 'Stopcock closed (a)'",
    "caption 'Stopcock open (b)'",
    "caption 'Some time after stopcock open (c)'",
    "legend mapping dot color to species (and, in mass mode, to molecular mass/speed)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "elapsedTime",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Elapsed time after opening",
      "range": [0, 1, 0.01],
      "default": 0,
      "teaches": "Dragging time from 0 to 1 sweeps continuously from fully separated gases, through an intermediate state where more H2 has crossed than O2, to a uniform mixture — showing mixing is gradual and gradient-driven, not instantaneous"
    },
    {
      "id": "playDiffusion",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Play diffusion",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "Watching the particles jitter and migrate on their own conveys that diffusion is an inherently dynamic, random-motion process that a single static snapshot cannot show"
    },
    {
      "id": "massColoring",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Color by",
      "options": ["Species", "Molecular mass"],
      "default": "Species",
      "teaches": "In mass mode the lighter H2 dots are tinted as 'fast' and the heavier O2 as 'slow', and the H2 side visibly empties sooner — making the mass-dependence of diffusion rate (Graham's law) explicit rather than implicit"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Stopcock closed",
      "narration": "With the valve shut, all white H2 dots stay in the left bulb and all red O2 dots in the right bulb — two sharp concentration gradients with no mixing.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 0, "massColoring": "Species" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Just opened",
      "narration": "Moments after opening, more white H2 dots have already crossed into the right bulb than red O2 dots have crossed left, because the lighter gas moves faster down its gradient.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 0.25, "massColoring": "Species" }
    },
    {
      "title": "See the mass effect",
      "narration": "Switch to color-by-mass at this same early time: the 'fast' light-gas tint has spread much further than the 'slow' heavy-gas tint, isolating the rate difference that species-coloring hides.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 0.25, "massColoring": "Molecular mass" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Equilibrium",
      "narration": "At full elapsed time both colors are spread roughly uniformly across both bulbs — the concentration gradients have equalized and net diffusion has stopped.",
      "state": { "elapsedTime": 1, "massColoring": "Species" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Diffusion runs down concentration gradients until uniform, and the lighter gas gets there first — the same mass-over-rate idea Graham's law quantifies.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two equal circular bulbs joined by a short horizontal neck with a small stopcock handle at its center; left bulb holds ~30 white H2 dots, right bulb ~30 red O2 dots. At t=0 the neck is blocked (handle vertical/closed) and each gas is confined to its bulb; as t increases the handle rotates open and dots migrate, with white dots crossing earlier and in greater number than red dots; at t=1 both bulbs hold an even white/red mix. Captions (a)/(b)/(c) sit beneath, but here they are driven by the single time slider rather than three fixed panels. In 'Molecular mass' toggle mode recolor white dots to a bright 'fast' hue (e.g. cyan) and red dots to a darker 'slow' hue (e.g. deep orange) and bias the migration so the fast hue spreads noticeably further at intermediate times. Keep dot counts and bulb sizes identical across modes so only color and position change."
}

